WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:04.910
- Bae, squad, fleek, basic,
no cap, bet, yaaaaas,

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:09.400
turn up, spilling tea, got
receipts, keep it 100, finesse.

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:11.250
These are just a few terms and phrases

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:12.810
that originated in black communities

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:14.310
And have become popularized--

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:18.470
Some might say co-opted--by
mainstream American culture,

00:00:18.470 --> 00:00:21.130
but this trend predates
the internet by decades.

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:23.330
Americans have long embraced contributions

00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:25.860
to popular language
from African-Americans,

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:27.300
arguably more than they've been willing

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:29.300
to embrace the people themselves.

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:31.800
This cultural dissonance is just one part

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:35.750
of the long complicated history
of African-American English.

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:38.870
And to unpack it, I'm
joined by sociolinguist

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:42.800
and AAE researcher, Dr.
Rachel Elizabeth Weissler.

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:45.320
- AAE can go by several different names:

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:47.030
African-American Language,

00:00:47.030 --> 00:00:50.770
African-American Vernacular
English, and Black English.

00:00:50.770 --> 00:00:52.950
"Ebonics" was coined in 1973

00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:55.760
by African-American
psychologist Robert Williams.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:58.700
But since then, it has been
co-opted by white speakers

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:00.060
as a derogatory term

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:02.960
and thus is not used by
language researchers.

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:05.340
Despite the Internet's
obsession with slang,

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:09.130
AAE is a lot more than a
collection of colloquialisms.

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:11.750
It is a complete and distinct sociolect,

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:15.420
a dialect created by social
rather than regional boundaries.

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:18.310
It is perhaps one of the most
culturally impactful varieties

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:21.632
of English on earth, but
also the most misunderstood.

00:01:29.076 --> 00:01:30.243
- Other words.

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:36.530
- In 1619, the English privateer
ship, white lion landed

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:38.520
at Point Comfort in Virginia.

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:41.060
Onboard were 20 enslaved
Africans who were traded

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:42.360
for supplies.

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:44.480
This transaction is of great historical

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:47.230
and linguistic significance,
because it marks the beginning

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:49.760
of slavery in British colonial America,

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:52.210
and the beginning of
African-American English.

00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:54.300
- The millions of people
kidnapped from Africa

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:56.500
and forced into slavery
in North America came

00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:59.200
from more than 45 distinct ethnic groups

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:01.280
and spoke many different languages.

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:02.960
In most cases, they couldn't communicate

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:05.060
with each other or their captors,

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:07.560
but communication is
necessary for survival.

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:09.700
And in this language contact situation,

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:12.010
the enslaved people were
forced to learn the language

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:14.130
of their captors, made
all the more difficult

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:17.190
by the fact that they were
not allowed to read or write.

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:20.640
In adopting English, heritage
languages were slowly lost,

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:22.530
though some words did manage to survive

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:27.000
in our lexicon to this day,
such as yam, banjo and okra.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:30.360
- There are several theories
about the foundations of AAE.

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:32.460
Perhaps it began as a pidgin,

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:34.660
a hastily developed form of communication

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:37.750
between groups that don't
share a common tongue,

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:40.650
or maybe it was the result of
English words being dropped

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:42.650
into African grammar systems.

00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:44.520
But the most popular theory

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:46.740
is that it grew out of the
English dialect spoken

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:49.490
by white rural southerners of the time.

00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:53.280
In this region, slavery and
then segregation kept black

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:54.930
and white community separated

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:56.560
creating an environment favorable

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:59.670
to maintaining distinct
varieties of English.

00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:00.890
In the 20th century,

00:03:00.890 --> 00:03:03.770
millions of African-American
seeking opportunities

00:03:03.770 --> 00:03:06.440
and fleeing racial persecution relocated

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:08.560
to large metropolitan areas in the north

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:10.240
known as The Great Migration,

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:14.080
which is why today AAE is
associated with urban areas.

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:17.220
There is a glaring misconception
that AAE is a wrong

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:18.490
or deficient version

00:03:18.490 --> 00:03:20.810
of what people call
Mainstream American English

00:03:20.810 --> 00:03:23.330
or Standardized American English,

00:03:23.330 --> 00:03:25.120
but that isn't the case at all.

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:26.740
And when it comes to language,

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:29.810
views about right and
wrong are often forms

00:03:29.810 --> 00:03:31.560
of social prejudice.

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:35.540
AAE has its own rules of
grammar, usage and pronunciation

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:38.920
that are as elegant and
consistent as any other dialect.

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:41.090
And contrary to negative stereotypes,

00:03:41.090 --> 00:03:44.420
speakers of AAE are not
careless with language.

00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:46.520
Like all people, they're very aware

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:48.470
of the rules of their native dialect

00:03:48.470 --> 00:03:51.270
and instantly know when
they're broken or misused.

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:53.430
- Are you sure that you wanna get turned

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:54.870
with your girls this weekend?

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:58.410
- I promised them a free
trip and it's turnt with a T

00:03:58.410 --> 00:04:00.370
- No matter what kind
of English you speak,

00:04:00.370 --> 00:04:01.700
you're going to sound different if you're

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:04.440
from New York City,
Atlanta, or Philadelphia,

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:07.630
and the regional phonetic
variation in AAE reflects that.

00:04:07.630 --> 00:04:10.980
However, there are some
common phonological features.

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:14.580
TH stopping, where a
D sound replaces a TH,

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:18.030
so that this and that
might be dis and dat.

00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:20.710
TH can also be replaced by an F or a V,

00:04:20.710 --> 00:04:24.280
so nothing and smooth
might be noffing and smoov.

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:27.150
Consonant cluster reduction,
where multiple continents

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:29.690
at the end of a word
are simplified into one.

00:04:29.690 --> 00:04:34.420
So for example, lift and test
might sound like lif and tes.

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:37.200
The common pronunciation of ask as aks

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:39.120
is an example of metathesis

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:42.390
in which two speech sounds
are allowed in flexible order.

00:04:42.390 --> 00:04:45.130
Many English words were
derived through metathesis.

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:49.060
For example, bird and horse
used to be bridd and hros.

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:51.160
Several others are
currently undergoing it,

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:54.270
like nucular, introduce and prescription.

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:57.560
- In addition to sound
features, AAE often differs

00:04:57.560 --> 00:05:00.320
from other types of
American English in grammar,

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:02.900
which is fairly constant
across the country.

00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:06.100
One of the most maligned
features of AAE's grammar

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:07.950
is the so-called double negative.

00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:10.200
As in, I ain't see nobody,

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:12.360
but this disparagement
reflects a misunderstanding

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:14.060
of how language works.

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:15.790
Language isn't like math,

00:05:15.790 --> 00:05:18.090
where two negatives cancel each other out.

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:21.590
In many languages like
Spanish, Greek and Hebrew,

00:05:21.590 --> 00:05:23.670
a second negative serves to confirm

00:05:23.670 --> 00:05:27.550
or amplify the negation
known as negative concord.

00:05:27.550 --> 00:05:30.760
For instance, the Spanish
sentence "No vi a nadie,"

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:34.250
literally translates
to "I didn't see nobody."

00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:36.640
Even standardized American
English employs a bit

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:38.400
of negative Concord.

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:40.740
If you wanna make this sentence negative,

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:43.700
you have to adjust the
verb and the object.

00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:46.920
The "anybody" serves the
same function as "nobody,"

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:49.910
to remind the listener that
the statement is negative.

00:05:49.910 --> 00:05:51.440
- Some other grammatical features

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:55.000
of AAE include: possessive S absence.

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:58.240
Instead of "the man's hat" or "my mama's car,"

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:01.230
you'd have "the man hat" or "my mama car."

00:06:01.230 --> 00:06:02.660
Question word order.

00:06:02.660 --> 00:06:04.840
Instead of "why aren't they going?"

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:07.047
You might hear "why they ain't going."

00:06:07.047 --> 00:06:09.250
"Finna", which etymologists trace back

00:06:09.250 --> 00:06:11.240
to the Southern phrase "fixing to"

00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:13.510
might replace "going to" or "gonna."

00:06:13.510 --> 00:06:15.220
"I'm going to go to the store,"

00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:17.550
might be "I'm finna go to the store." 

00:06:17.550 --> 00:06:21.210
Zero copula, the connecting
verb "to be" can be removed

00:06:21.210 --> 00:06:23.720
from sentences where they
would form a contraction.

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:26.680
So, "who is that" might become "who dat,"

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:30.480
and "she's my sister" might
become "she my sister."

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:32.380
Other languages like Japanese and Russian

00:06:32.380 --> 00:06:34.290
also employs the zero copula.

00:06:34.290 --> 00:06:38.720
For instance, "ona doma" literally
translates to "she at home."

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:41.019
No S on third person singular.

00:06:41.019 --> 00:06:45.020
MAE changes form only for
third person singular verbs.

00:06:45.020 --> 00:06:49.110
I run, you run, she
runs, we run, they run,

00:06:49.110 --> 00:06:50.850
only one S in there.

00:06:50.850 --> 00:06:54.290
AAE keeps all verb forms
consistent and regular.

00:06:54.290 --> 00:06:55.700
In many cases,

00:06:55.700 --> 00:06:59.350
AEs verb conjugation can
actually achieve more nuance

00:06:59.350 --> 00:07:02.340
and precision than
mainstream American English.

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:04.610
When I say "she is working,"

00:07:04.610 --> 00:07:08.830
I generally mean one of two
things, either she has a job

00:07:08.830 --> 00:07:11.260
or she is currently doing work right now.

00:07:11.260 --> 00:07:14.180
In AAE, those are differentiated.

00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:16.860
"She working" means that
she is currently working

00:07:16.860 --> 00:07:20.370
at this moment, and "she be
working" means that she works

00:07:20.370 --> 00:07:22.060
on a regular basis.

00:07:22.060 --> 00:07:24.770
And the third option, "she been working."

00:07:24.770 --> 00:07:27.290
She is working and has been for a while.

00:07:27.290 --> 00:07:30.090
- Now, some of these features
also occur in other varieties

00:07:30.090 --> 00:07:32.380
of English and not all of them are used

00:07:32.380 --> 00:07:34.680
by every speaker of AAE.

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:37.760
But taken together, they index AAE,

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:39.740
which means they serve
as a sign that points

00:07:39.740 --> 00:07:42.320
to a shared history and cultural identity.

00:07:42.320 --> 00:07:44.270
Because of history is
fraught with tension,

00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:46.980
discrimination and violence,
it's not surprising

00:07:46.980 --> 00:07:49.870
that AAE is considered
a controversial topic.

00:07:49.870 --> 00:07:53.180
It is the most broadly studied
variety of American English

00:07:53.180 --> 00:07:56.400
and has been the subject of
many high profile court cases

00:07:56.400 --> 00:07:59.610
over issues like school instruction
and linguistic profiling

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:01.940
in employment and criminal justice.

00:08:01.940 --> 00:08:04.040
Attitudes of stigma and language prejudice

00:08:04.040 --> 00:08:07.850
have forced many speakers
of AAE to be bidialectal,

00:08:07.850 --> 00:08:10.740
able to speak both AAE
and whatever's considered

00:08:10.740 --> 00:08:12.900
the mainstream norm of their region.

00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:16.400
Alternating between two
different dialects or languages

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:20.600
or social linguistic practices
is called "code switching."

00:08:20.600 --> 00:08:23.660
It can occur casually, the
way bilingual children

00:08:23.660 --> 00:08:25.700
of Spanish speaking
parents will transition

00:08:25.700 --> 00:08:29.780
from English to Spanish, back
to English in one sentence.

00:08:29.780 --> 00:08:32.750
But it can also be socially mandated.

00:08:32.750 --> 00:08:35.750
Say, if you speak in a
formal register of Japanese

00:08:35.750 --> 00:08:38.620
with many honorifics
when addressing your boss

00:08:38.620 --> 00:08:40.600
versus the more colloquial
language you may use

00:08:40.600 --> 00:08:41.840
with your pals.

00:08:41.840 --> 00:08:45.380
- Most speakers of AAE are
adept at switching to MAE based

00:08:45.380 --> 00:08:47.720
on context and setting
like a job interview

00:08:47.720 --> 00:08:49.699
or other professional situation.

00:08:49.831 --> 00:08:51.150
- You wanna make some money here?

00:08:51.150 --> 00:08:52.522
Use your white voice.

00:08:52.522 --> 00:08:53.470
- My white voice.

00:08:53.470 --> 00:08:56.960
- Hey, Mr. Kramer, this is
Langston from Regal View.

00:08:56.960 --> 00:08:59.570
- But this skill is not
without its struggles.

00:08:59.570 --> 00:09:02.070
Having to constantly
scan social situations

00:09:02.070 --> 00:09:03.610
and adjust one speech patterns

00:09:03.610 --> 00:09:05.530
is a layer of extra mental effort

00:09:05.530 --> 00:09:08.120
that white speakers don't
have to worry about.

00:09:08.120 --> 00:09:10.370
And speakers of AAE may face blow back

00:09:10.370 --> 00:09:12.650
from their own communities
when code switching,

00:09:12.650 --> 00:09:14.730
being accused of trying to sound white.

00:09:14.730 --> 00:09:18.390
- You never wanna be the
whitest sounding black guy

00:09:18.390 --> 00:09:19.223
in a room.

00:09:19.223 --> 00:09:20.100
- There's also the question

00:09:20.100 --> 00:09:22.770
of why they should have to
change the way they speak at all

00:09:22.770 --> 00:09:25.290
just to make others more comfortable.

00:09:25.290 --> 00:09:26.650
It can be frustrating to know

00:09:26.650 --> 00:09:28.850
that despite the linguistic evidence,

00:09:28.850 --> 00:09:31.760
many Americans still
hear AAE as an inferior

00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:34.420
or wrong version of
mainstream American English.

00:09:34.420 --> 00:09:37.430
- And yet ironically, AAE is also one

00:09:37.430 --> 00:09:40.850
of the most copied and
co-opted English dialects.

00:09:40.850 --> 00:09:43.600
Some non-black speakers might
use features of the dialect

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:46.170
to show affiliation with African-Americans

00:09:46.170 --> 00:09:48.690
or with traits they might
stereotypically associate

00:09:48.690 --> 00:09:51.913
with them, like confidence,
coolness or toughness.

00:09:51.913 --> 00:09:55.360
♪ Yeah, I keep my TEC-9 on my waist
cause I'm a gangster ♪

00:09:55.360 --> 00:09:57.030
- I know they don't mean any harm,

00:09:57.030 --> 00:09:59.630
but they don't realize how ignorant it is.

00:09:59.630 --> 00:10:04.397
Trying to have that black voice
that some whites try to do,

00:10:04.397 --> 00:10:06.050
but they really don't do well.

00:10:06.050 --> 00:10:07.380
- There are people like myself

00:10:07.380 --> 00:10:09.060
who do know what they're
saying and could do it.

00:10:09.060 --> 00:10:10.841
I think, I honestly think I could.

00:10:10.841 --> 00:10:13.010
(audience laughing)

00:10:13.010 --> 00:10:16.600
- Both American pop culture
and youth culture rely on

00:10:16.600 --> 00:10:19.520
and are shaped by black
cultural practices,

00:10:19.520 --> 00:10:23.020
products and behaviors,
including music and language.

00:10:23.020 --> 00:10:25.150
As these cultural elements radiate outward

00:10:25.150 --> 00:10:27.580
from urban centers to
the rest of the world,

00:10:27.580 --> 00:10:30.990
slang expressions that
originated in AAE are constantly

00:10:30.990 --> 00:10:33.090
being adopted by other groups, groups

00:10:33.090 --> 00:10:35.810
that don't necessarily
understand the background,

00:10:35.810 --> 00:10:37.800
context and appropriate usage,

00:10:37.800 --> 00:10:39.930
but enjoy the social clout they bring.

00:10:39.930 --> 00:10:42.410
- AAE is a part of the
cultural inheritance

00:10:42.410 --> 00:10:44.230
of many African-Americans.

00:10:44.230 --> 00:10:46.720
And the strong sense of cultural identity

00:10:46.720 --> 00:10:50.470
is also one of oppositionality,
of embracing the contrast

00:10:50.470 --> 00:10:53.220
with other groups and
broader white society.

00:10:53.220 --> 00:10:56.800
The history that shaped AAE
has more than its fair share

00:10:56.800 --> 00:10:59.430
of tragedy and violence,
the societal effects

00:10:59.430 --> 00:11:01.570
of which we are still living with today.

00:11:01.570 --> 00:11:04.290
But the negative associations
that many people attach

00:11:04.290 --> 00:11:08.360
to AAE cause us to overlook
the triumphs, perseverance,

00:11:08.360 --> 00:11:11.260
and creativity that gave
African-American culture

00:11:11.260 --> 00:11:14.250
an outsized role on the world stage.

00:11:14.250 --> 00:11:17.160
Language, it turns out
is more a reflection

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:18.880
of the overcoming of obstacles

00:11:18.880 --> 00:11:20.563
than the obstacles themselves.

00:11:21.654 --> 00:11:22.663
- Do what, what are you talking?

00:11:22.663 --> 00:11:25.610
- I think I could write
for you in your vernacular

00:11:25.610 --> 00:11:26.443
because of-

00:11:26.443 --> 00:11:27.276
- But what is my vernacular?

00:11:27.276 --> 00:11:29.240
- Well, because I hang out with-

00:11:29.240 --> 00:11:30.900
- Yeah, but what is my vernacular?

00:11:30.900 --> 00:11:31.733
What are you talking about?

00:11:31.733 --> 00:11:34.020
- Well, like this hog is all on my yoke.

00:11:34.020 --> 00:11:35.726
- Right, Dick, what are you talking about?

00:11:35.726 --> 00:11:38.559
(audience laughs)

